    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `fill_gaps` in `app/gaps.py` and added tests for it. The full suite passes (7 tests: the 2 that were already there plus 5 new ones).\n\n**How it works:**\n1. It copies the input, so the original frame is never changed.\n2. It groups `temp_c` by `station` and fills each gap with that station's most recent earlier temperature.\n3. It then groups again and fills anything still missing with the station's first later temperature.\n4. The result is written back by row position rather than by index label, so the fill still lands on the right rows if the index has duplicates or is out of order.\n\nValues are only ever taken from the same station, so a station with no temperatures at all keeps its missing values. The row order, the index and the other columns come back exactly as they were given.\n\n**One case the spec didn't cover:** if a row has no `station` value at all, it isn't treated as belonging to any station, so its missing `temp_c` stays missing. I chose that to stay consistent with \"never use another station's values.\"\n\n**New tests** (in `tests/test_gaps.py`) cover:\n- filling from an earlier value\n- filling a leading gap from a later value\n- a station with no temperatures\n- a row with no station\n- that the input is unchanged and the order, index and other columns are preserved, including with a duplicated index\n\nNo dependencies were added."
